WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.189
Hi, I'm Tucker.

00:00:01.452 --> 00:00:04.754
This is a short walk through PA.gov, the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania's website,

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:06.682
and the part I played in it.

00:00:06.938 --> 00:00:10.316
I owned the UX architecture and led a team of four designers.

00:00:10.316 --> 00:00:12.392
I also set direction for our visual design lead,

00:00:12.543 --> 00:00:14.323
a fellow associate creative director.

00:00:14.527 --> 00:00:16.982
We worked closely with the governor's digital team.

00:00:17.495 --> 00:00:19.677
The program covered sixty-two websites across forty-three agencies,

00:00:19.677 --> 00:00:22.840
each organized around the agency that ran it.

00:00:23.242 --> 00:00:25.335
The governor had committed to "no wrong door."

00:00:25.564 --> 00:00:26.709
The sites were doing the opposite.

00:00:27.423 --> 00:00:28.912
We didn't guess at this.

00:00:28.912 --> 00:00:33.381
Over ten months, we talked with and tested with 566 residents across three life events,

00:00:33.381 --> 00:00:35.813
including screen reader users and people living with cognitive disabilities.

00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:40.351
A first-time applicant is often coming off a financial shock,

00:00:40.351 --> 00:00:43.032
and gets lost in complex terms and unreliable sites.

00:00:43.032 --> 00:00:45.125
People already on benefits struggle to keep them,

00:00:45.547 --> 00:00:48.123
with renewals that are easy to miss.

00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:49.481
Both pointed to one starting point:

00:00:49.855 --> 00:00:54.344
help people find the right service without knowing which agency owns it.

00:00:54.914 --> 00:00:56.430
Here's the structure, simplified.

00:00:56.691 --> 00:00:59.038
Services come first, as task-based entry points,

00:00:59.038 --> 00:01:01.980
with the content supplied by each agency.

00:01:02.335 --> 00:01:06.521
Then every agency gets the same landing page, with the same seven sections.

00:01:06.655 --> 00:01:09.794
Visitors learn the pattern once, and it works on every agency's site.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.202
The core idea was to start from the resident, not the org chart.

00:01:13.644 --> 00:01:17.715
On Find Services, life-event filters sit next to agency and service type.

00:01:17.841 --> 00:01:21.756
Choose "Navigating Unemployment," and you see services from every agency at once.

00:01:22.166 --> 00:01:24.389
That works because we moved content from folders,

00:01:24.389 --> 00:01:27.698
one tree per agency, to tags for agency, topic, and life event.

00:01:27.698 --> 00:01:31.283
So one service page shows up wherever a resident might look for it.

00:01:31.754 --> 00:01:35.471
The tree tests told us the structure mostly worked, but the words didn't.

00:01:35.797 --> 00:01:38.670
Half of the patients and caregivers we tested didn't know what "ECM" meant.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.123
I worked through the results with our researcher

00:01:40.442 --> 00:01:43.959
and made the final call on the labels.

00:01:44.115 --> 00:01:46.560
The rule was simple: name what a resident is looking for,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.035
not the office that owns it.

00:01:48.524 --> 00:01:50.991
We also gave every service the same shape.

00:01:50.991 --> 00:01:53.907
It opens with what the service is and how to start,

00:01:54.089 --> 00:01:58.459
then an on-page guide to eligibility, filing, and next steps.

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:00.801
The ways to act sit side by side: online,

00:02:00.871 --> 00:02:04.897
by phone, or by videophone for people who use sign language.

00:02:05.356 --> 00:02:06.973
I also pitched a guided search: a few simple questions

00:02:07.328 --> 00:02:10.648
that lead to the services you may be eligible for.

00:02:10.888 --> 00:02:14.570
Residents had asked for exactly that in our survey, and it tested well.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:16.534
It wasn't in scope, and it wasn't built.

00:02:17.231 --> 00:02:20.391
One more decision: we first planned to organize around life events,

00:02:20.391 --> 00:02:23.099
but no one on the client side was going to own that content.

00:02:23.602 --> 00:02:27.064
So we kept our recommendations simpler, and put the effort

00:02:27.064 --> 00:02:29.660
into examples and guidance agencies could follow on their own.

00:02:30.245 --> 00:02:31.311
That turned out to matter.

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:33.888
Agencies weren't required to adopt the new structure,

00:02:33.888 --> 00:02:35.383
and all but a handful did.

00:02:35.825 --> 00:02:40.275
Across the program, the accessibility audit score went from 37 to 95 percent.

00:02:40.566 --> 00:02:42.892
Those numbers come from a lot of people's work.

00:02:43.145 --> 00:02:45.081
My part was the structure underneath,

00:02:45.199 --> 00:02:47.274
the words on it, and the team that designed it.

00:02:47.505 --> 00:02:48.305
Thanks for watching.
